## ABSTRACT
The use of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as solid adsorption materials for carbon capture is promising, but achieving efficient and reversible adsorption with a balance of capacity and selectivity for carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\) over \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) remains a challenge. To take full advantage of the strong channel traffic and robustness of MOFs with relatively small pores, it is highly necessary to employ a defect-engineering strategy to construct a broader channel structure that can facilitate the loading of functional motif-rich amino acids (AAs). This strategy can greatly enhance the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption performance of MOF. In this study, motif-rich amino acids are loaded into the defective and robust porous frameworks via combined defect-engineering and postsynthetic methods. The defective Zr/Hf-MOF-808s modified with AAs, especially for the \(18\;\;\mathrm{mol}\%\) 4-nitroisophthalic acid, generated defective products allowing for the loading of L-serine (L-Ser). This modification resulted in a significant improvement in both the adsorption capacity (248% improvement at \(298\;\mathsf{K},\;100\;\mathsf{kPa}\)) and the selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) using the ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST), with the selectivity increasing to 120.55 and 38.27 at 15 and \(100\;\mathsf{kPa}\), respectively, while maintaining good cycling performance. Density functional theory (DFT) simulation, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) temperature-programmed desorption \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\)-TPD), and in situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) were further employed to have a better understanding of the enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity. Interestingly, unlike the AAs loaded pristine MOF-808s that showed the best \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity with the loading of short and small glycine (Gly), the broadened channel size in our work enables the loading of functional motif-rich L-serine, which brings more active binding sites, improving \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption.
## KEYWORDS
carbon capture and storage, metal-organic framework (MOF), amino acids (AAs), defect-engineering, motif-rich
## 1 Introduction
The rise of carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\) levels in the atmosphere highlights the importance of improving current carbon capture and storage (CCS) technology [1, 2]. Based on the consensus, various materials such as carbons [3, 4], zeolites [5], silica [6], and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [7–12] have been developed. Among these, MOFs are highly promising materials for CCS due to their large surface area, adjustable structures, and variable channel shape and motifs [13–18], which are key factors in gas handling operations that use membrane separation or pressure swing adsorption technologies.
The efficient capture and selective separation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is crucial for mitigating the greenhouse effect. Currently, it is challenging to achieve efficient and reversible adsorption while balancing
capacity and selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) [7, 19]. One effective method to improve \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture performance is by loading amines into MOFs to create additional active sites and modulate the channel traffic [20–22]. Amino acids (AAs) have garnered significant attention in numerous amine-based carbon capture systems due to their highly active and abundant adsorption sites, robustness against thermal and oxidative degradation, low volatility, high biodegradability, absence of toxic intermediates, and reduced corrosiveness [23, 24]. For example, a novel material of L-arginine (L-Arg) modified Cu-MOFs (Cu-MOF/graphene oxide (GO)-L-Arg) was synthesized. Cu-MOF/GO-L-Arg exhibited an improved \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity, which was about 46% higher than that of Cu-MOF/GO at \(50\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The increase in adsorption capacity was attributed to the additional adsorption sites provided by L-arginine [22]. To address the issue of
insufficient robustness and low selectivity caused by weak channel traffic in MOFs with relatively large pores, researchers have turned to MOFs with smaller pores as potential host materials. A recent study demonstrated that MOF-808, functionalized with 11 amino acids, has greatly improved adsorption capacity [25]. However, the study also indicated the best results obtained by AAs of the smallest size \(\sim\,500\ \mathrm{\pmn})\) . This suggests that there may be limitations in introducing additional functional motifs into the porous frameworks due to the relatively large size of some amino acids. As an effective strategy to both maintain small pores and broaden the channel size, defect-engineering can create defective channel pathways to facilitate highly effective loading of AAs with relatively large sizes (e.g., L-serine (L-Ser), \(\sim\,900\ \mathrm{\pmn})\) , while maintaining the major frameworks.
In this study, defective bimetallic \(Z\mathrm{r/Hf-MOF-808s}\) are prepared to generate well-accessible cavities, facilitating the loading of AAs with relatively large sizes and rich functional motifs. These motif-rich AAs loaded defective MOFs provide a platform to study the structure–property relationships involving defect construction, functional motif modulation, and the resulting active adsorption sites. The mechanistic aspects of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding sites are explored using density functional theory (DFT) simulation calculations, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) temperature-programmed desorption \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) -TPD), and in situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR).
## 2 Results and discussion
MOF-808s are made up of zirconium oxide or hafnium oxide clusters and 1,3,5-benzene dicarboxylate linkers to yield a three-dimensional porous rigid skeleton (Fig. 1(a)).
Zirconium oxide clusters consist of six \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{8}\) tetragonal antiprisms linked by an oxygen atom, while the zirconium atom is body-centered. On the periphery of the six \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{8}\) tetragonal antiprisms are six 1,3,5-benzene dicarboxylate linkers that are connected to oxygen atoms of the zirconium oxide clusters. Even though the pristine \(Z\mathrm{r-MOF-808}\) and Hf-MOF-808 do not have open metal sites, defective \(Z\mathrm{r/Hf-MOF-808s}\) enable the expansion of the pore size and the addition of other carbon dioxideattracting groups via the unsaturated carboxyl groups (Fig. 1(b)). In addition to the joints between metal sites and organic ligands, other sites are occupied by formic acid ligands in the form of a bidentate or monodentate binding mode, which can be occupied by carboxylic acid derivatives to establish AAs-defective \(Z\mathrm{r/Hf-}\) MOF-808s (Fig. 1(c)).
Compared with pristine \(Z\mathrm{r-MOF-808}\) , the bimetal \(Z\mathrm{r/Hf-MOF-}\) 808 exhibits different thermal stabilities and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption properties. The doping of Hf did not alter the crystal structure
As illustrated in Fig. 2(a), XRD patterns implied that an \(18\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) of organic ligand deficiency did not destroy the crystal structure, thereby allowing for the expansion of pore size and additional loading of large amino acids (Fig. S15 in the ESM) [27]. Thermogravimetric loss, evaluated by a thermal gravimetric analyzer, is a vital parameter to estimate the thermal stability of defective structures [28]. As is depicted in Fig. 2(b), compared with pristine \(Z\mathrm{r-MOF-808}\) (Zr-M), \(Z\mathrm{r/Hf}\) (9:1)-MOF-808 (Zr/Hf (9:1)-M) displayed a higher thermal decomposition temperature, implying that bimetallic ligands enhance the thermal stability of the skeleton structure [29]. Notably, \(Z\mathrm{r/Hf}\) (9:1)-MOF-808-4- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-IPA (18%) (Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-IPA (33%)) demonstrated a higher thermal decomposition temperature and minimal
thermogravimetric loss, highlighting its improved thermal stability [30]. Furthermore, Fig. 2(c) confirms that Zr-M, Zr/Hf (9:1)-M, and defective Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4-NO\(_2\)-IPA (18%) possess a regular octahedral structure, with slight morphological variations resulting from the doping of defects, as supported by the XRD patterns [31].
AAs-defective Zr/Hf-MOF-808s were obtained through the post-synthetic incorporation of amino acids into defective Zr/Hf-MOF-808s [32]. AAs were selected to be blended in defective Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4-NO\(_2\)-IPA (18%), including L-serine (L-Ser), L-proline (L-Pro), glycine (Gly), L-cysteine (L-Cys), L-glutamic acid (L-Glu), L-alanine (L-Ala), and L-arginine. Of all incorporated AAs, L-serine (0.3 mol·L\(^{-1}\)) demonstrated the highest CO\(_2\) adsorption capacity (Figs. S12 and S13 in the ESM), reaching 0.81 and 2.41 mmol·g\(^{-1}\) at 15 and 100 kPa, respectively. As mentioned above, the doping of 4-NO\(_2\)-IPA would decrease the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET)-specific surface area slightly but enlarge the pore size which is conducive to amino acid loading (Figs. S14 and S15 in the ESM, N\(_2\) adsorption desorption isotherm, and pore size distribution) [33,34]. The facilitated modification of AAs resulted in channel surfaces rich in functional motifs, which may explain the slight increase in CO\(_2\) adsorption observed upon the blocking of pore structures by amino acid loading [35]. As expected, the introduction of amino acids increased the adsorption quantity of CO\(_2\) [36]. As is depicted in Fig. 3(a), the CO\(_2\) adsorption capacity at 100 kPa follows the sequence: Zr-M (0.95 mmol·g\(^{-1}\)) < Zr/Hf (9:1)-M (1.14 mmol·g\(^{-1}\)) < Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4-NO\(_2\)-IPA (2.06 mmol·g\(^{-1}\)) < L-Ser-Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4-NO\(_2\)-IPA (2.41 mmol·g\(^{-1}\)). This trend can be attributed to the favorable contributions of loading L-Ser and -NO\(_2\) groups [37–39].
To evaluate the CO\(_2\)/N\(_2\) selectivity of the MOF-808s sorbents, the ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) selectivity has been calculated (for details see Eq. (S1) in the ESM). Figure 3(b) indicates that modification with amino acids improves CO\(_2\)/N\(_2\)
selectivity from 15.30 to 38.27 at 100 kPa, which is 2.50 times than that of pristine Zr-MOF-808. Notably, in the low-pressure stage, the CO\(_2\)/N\(_2\) selectivity is greater at 120.55 at 15 kPa. To characterize the adsorption heat (Q) during the adsorption process, CO\(_2\) adsorption isotherms were evaluated at 273 and 298 K based on the Clausius–Clapeyron relation (for details see Eq. (S2) in the ESM). Figure 3(c) shows that Zr/Hf(9:1)-M-NO\(_2\)-IPA (18%) and L-Ser-Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-NO\(_2\)-IPA (18%) have higher adsorption heats than Zr/Hf (9:1)-M, with values of 22.5 and 24.0 kJ·mol\(^{-1}\), respectively, which is consistent with their CO\(_2\) adsorption performance. The adsorbed quantities of CO\(_2\) were dependent mainly on pore structure, nitro groups, and amino acids which are crucial for selective CO\(_2\) capture [40,41].
To further understand the role of the defect sites [42,43], DFT was utilized to study the adsorption sites and adsorption energies of CO\(_2\) into the synthesized MOFs (details see Eq. (S3) in the ESM) [44,45]. The primitive unit cell of Zr-MOF-808, which was used in previous calculations, was employed in these simulations. The adsorption sites and adsorption energies were calculated based on actual CO\(_2\) adsorption capacity [46]. Table 1 shows that the defective structures enhanced CO\(_2\) adsorption with adsorption energies ranging from 27.5 to 42.0 kJ·mol\(^{-1}\), as demonstrated by DFT calculations. Moreover, the defective Zr/Hf-MOF-808 possesses the highest adsorption energy [47,48]. Notably, site II and site III possess higher binding energy after being modified with 4-NO\(_2\)-IPA, increasing by 1.30 and 3.10 kJ·mol\(^{-1}\), respectively. The enhanced CO\(_2\) affinity brought by the modification of 4-NO\(_2\)-IPA is supported by the observed increase in CO\(_2\) adsorption capacity [49,50].
As shown in Fig. 4, adsorption sites mainly lie in site I, site II, site III, and site IV. Site I and site II are found in the small cavity consisting of four Zr-O clusters connected by 1,3,5-benzene dicarboxylate linkers, while site III and site IV are found in the large cavity. In particular, the presence of 4-NO\(_2\)-IPA significantly strengthens the adsorption of CO\(_2\) at site III and site III.
In addition, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \)-TPD was conducted to demonstrate the number of alkaline sites [50, 51]. Regarding \( \mathrm{Zr/Hf} \) (9:1)-M, the peak sites are observed at 278 and \( 400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), indicating that the channel structure’s surface acts as a medium strong alkali center. L-Ser-Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4- \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \)-IPA \( (18\%) \) possesses more alkaline sites, distributed at 116, 287, and \( 432\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) (Figs. S19 and S20 in the ESM, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \)-TPD). The emergence of a new peak temperature at \( 116\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and the increase of peak temperature to \( 432\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) represent an increase in the number of alkaline central sites [52, 53]. Compared to \( \mathrm{Zr/Hf} \) (9:1)-M, L-Ser-Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4- \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \)-IPA further increases the amount of strong alkaline centers by the construction of defects and modification of L-Ser which is in line with the observed \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption performance [54].
