To investigate the mechanism of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption in L-Ser-Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4- \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \)-IPA \( (18\%) \), in situ FTIR was conducted. As shown in Fig. S21 in the ESM, with the increase of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption time, new peaks at 2346 and \( 2378\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) appear, which can be attributed to \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) physical adsorption. Similarly, two peaks at 1685 and \( 1494\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) emerge gradually in response to carbamate formation indicating strong \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption. In the desorption process (Fig. S22 in the ESM, in situ FTIR of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) desorption), the peaks at \( 1013\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) and \( 1286\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) do not completely disappear, which correspond to residual carbonate [55, 56].
Based on the analysis of in situ FTIR and DFT simulation results, we proposed a possible mechanism of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption in L-Ser-Zr/Hf (9:1)-M-4- \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \)-IPA \( (18\%) \). As illustrated in Fig. 5, the additional adsorption sites of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) are mainly located in the \( -\mathrm{NO}_{2} \) group, defective sites, and functional motifs of amino acids [57, 58]. The presence of defective organic ligands, such as \( 4-\mathrm{NO}_{2} \)-IPA, enhances \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption performance by electrostatic interaction due to the polar nitro group, which strengthens quadrupole–dipole interactions and the defect-induced open metal sites [59]. As for amino acids, the high affinity of such functional motifs
provides strong and abundant adsorption sites. Therefore, the defective sites and functional motifs cooperatively improve the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity [60]. Interestingly, in contrast to the previously reported \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity enhancement in pristine MOF-808s upon loading with short and small Gly, our work indicates that the defect-engineering is an effective strategy to broaden channel size to facilitate the loading of functional motif-rich L-Ser, resulting in the creation of more active binding sites in a confined space.
As presented in Fig. 6, the selected adsorbent, L-Ser-Zr/Hf (9:1)- M-4- \(\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -IPA \((18\%)\) , demonstrates robustness and reusability for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture, confirmed by its usage for up to 5 cycles following a straightforward reactivation at \(423\ \mathrm{K}\) under vacuum for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) [61]. After 5 cycles, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity remains at \(86.17\%\) , indicating that amino acids modified defective MOF-808s possess good cycling performance.
## 3 Conclusions
The incorporation of amino acids into the defective Zr/Hf-MOF808s, particularly with the addition of \(18\;\;\mathrm{mol}\%\) \(4{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -IPA, resulted in a defective framework with L-serine loaded into the skeleton. This approach led to a significant increase in both the adsorption capacity \((248\%\) improvement at \(298\;\mathrm{~K~}\) ) and the selective removal of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (the ratio of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) from 15.30 to 38.27), as well as exhibiting good reusability. A bimetallic strategy has been utilized to construct robust Zr/Hf-MOF-808 frameworks compared with pristine samples. The appropriate amount of defect-engineering ligand, \(4{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -IPA, not only improves the capacity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption but also results in enlarged pore structures that facilitate the subsequent loading of functional motif-rich amino acids. DFT simulation was further
employed to better understand the enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption after \(4{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -IPA defect-engineering. Interestingly, unlike the AAs loaded pristine MOF-808s that showed the best \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity with the loading of short and small glycine (Figs. S23 and S24 in the ESM, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms), the broadened channel size in our work enables the loading of functional motif-rich L-serine, which provides more active binding sites and exhibits the best \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption. By introducing defects into a sturdy MOF structure and then loading amino acids with large motifs into the resulting pores, it becomes possible to adjust the channel structure to have a relatively larger aperture size without altering the original framework. This approach is expected to significantly improve the selective capture of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) during industrial production and manufacturing processes, as well as other gas handling operations [62–65].
